sw","cfikmsx","cfikmsy","cfikmsz","cfiknpw","cfiknpx","cfiknpy","cfiknpz","cfiknqw","cfiknqx","cfiknqy","cfiknqz","cfiknrw","cfiknrx","cfiknry","cfiknrz","cfiknsw","cfiknsx","cfiknsy","cfiknsz","cfikopw","cfikopx","cfikopy","cfikopz","cfikoqw","cfikoqx","cfikoqy","cfikoqz","cfikorw","cfikorx","cfikory","cfikorz","cfikosw","cfikosx","cfikosy","cfikosz","cfilmpw","cfilmpx","cfilmpy","cfilmpz","cfilmqw","cfilmqx","cfilmqy","cfilmqz","cfilmrw","cfilmrx","cfilmry","cfilmrz","cfilmsw","cfilmsx","cfilmsy","cfilmsz","cfilnpw","cfilnpx","cfilnpy","cfilnpz","cfilnqw","cfilnqx","cfilnqy","cfilnqz","cfilnrw","cfilnrx","cfilnry","cfilnrz","cfilnsw","cfilnsx","cfilnsy","cfilnsz","cfilopw","cfilopx","cfilopy","cfilopz","cfiloqw","cfiloqx","cfiloqy","cfiloqz","cfilorw","cfilorx","cfilory","cfilorz","cfilosw","cfilosx","cfilosy","cfilosz"]}],"solution":"class Solution:\n    def letterCombinations(self, digits: str) -> List[str]:\n        if not digits:\n            return []\n        keypad = {\"2\": \"abc\", \"3\": \"def\", \"4\": \"ghi\", \"5\": \"jkl\",\n                  \"6\": \"mno\", \"7\": \"pqrs\", \"8\": \"tuv\", \"9\": \"wxyz\"}\n        res = []\n        path = []\n\n        def backtrack(i: int) -> None:\n            if i == len(digits):\n                res.append(\"\".join(path))\n                return\n            for ch in keypad[digits[i]]:\n                path.append(ch)\n                backtrack(i + 1)\n                path.pop()\n\n        backtrack(0)\n        return res\n","approach":"**Key insight: the answer is the Cartesian product of the letter groups, one group per digit.** Backtracking over the digit index enumerates that product with O(L) extra memory.\n\n**Algorithm.** Map each digit to its letters. `backtrack(i)`: when `i == len(digits)` join the path and record it; otherwise for each letter of `digits[i]` push, recurse on `i + 1`, pop. Return `[]` immediately for empty input.\n\n**Why it works.** Each leaf fixes one letter per position, and distinct leaf paths give distinct strings, so every combination appears exactly once.\n\n**Common mistakes:** returning `[\"\"]` for empty input; building strings by concatenation at every level (fine for L <= 7 but O(L^2) per leaf in general); forgetting that 7 and 9 have four letters.\n\n**Interview follow-ups:** Iterative BFS version? (start with `[\"\"]` and for each digit extend every partial string.) Only count the combinations? (product of group sizes, O(L).) Filter to real dictionary words, as T9 does? (walk a trie of the dictionary alongside the digits and prune dead branches.)","complexity":{"time":"O(L * 4^L)","space":"O(L) excluding output"},"similar":["subsets","generate-parentheses","combinations"],"number":93,"starter":"class Solution:\n    def letterCombinations(self, digits: str) -> List[str]:\n        pass\n"}